1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:05,180
You know, usually when we talk about a medical diagnosis or, I mean, really any kind of structural

2
00:00:05,180 --> 00:00:09,220
assessment, there's this built-in expectation of precision.

3
00:00:09,640 --> 00:00:11,880
Absolutely. It's like engineering. You want a clear answer.

4
00:00:11,980 --> 00:00:16,360
Right, exactly. It's like you break your arm, the x-ray shows a jagged white line on the film,

5
00:00:16,540 --> 00:00:18,200
and the doctor just points at it.

6
00:00:18,260 --> 00:00:21,260
And they say, well, there it is. That's the problem. Broken or not broken.

7
00:00:21,460 --> 00:00:26,500
Yeah, and it's a very comforting binary. You know, it gives us a really clear path to fix it.

8
00:00:26,600 --> 00:00:27,960
We crave that visibility.

9
00:00:27,960 --> 00:00:35,820
We really do. But then you step into almost any deeply complex system, whether that's like

10
00:00:35,820 --> 00:00:39,340
neurodevelopment or macroeconomics or...

11
00:00:39,340 --> 00:00:42,420
Or even just trying to fix legacy code in a software program.

12
00:00:42,680 --> 00:00:45,940
Right. And suddenly that clean x-ray machine is completely useless.

13
00:00:46,340 --> 00:00:50,320
You're looking at a diagnostic landscape that is just, well, it's murky.

14
00:00:50,400 --> 00:00:53,040
You pull one thread and three others unravel.

15
00:00:53,340 --> 00:00:56,140
Because you're suddenly in the realm of cascading dependencies.

16
00:00:56,140 --> 00:00:58,700
You can't just isolate one thing.

17
00:00:59,060 --> 00:01:03,760
Because the thing is actually defined by its relationship to, you know, everything else around it.

18
00:01:03,920 --> 00:01:04,080
Mm-hmm.

19
00:01:04,320 --> 00:01:10,940
And that feeling, that vertigo of stepping out of a clean binary world of isolated objects

20
00:01:10,940 --> 00:01:12,980
and into something infinitely more tangled,

21
00:01:13,700 --> 00:01:17,900
that is exactly the feeling I got when I opened up the source material for today's Deep Dive.

22
00:01:17,900 --> 00:01:23,920
It is quite the document. For those listening, we are looking at a dense 65-page working draft.

23
00:01:24,040 --> 00:01:29,300
Yeah. It's dated June 10, 2026. It's titled The Flyxion Project Map.

24
00:01:29,300 --> 00:01:36,220
And, um, this document claims to outline the intellectual architecture of something called the admissibility program.

25
00:01:36,220 --> 00:01:38,960
Calling it ambitious might be a massive understatement.

26
00:01:39,500 --> 00:01:45,000
It is a full-scale attempt to remap, well, fundamental physics, how human cognition works,

27
00:01:45,360 --> 00:01:50,520
computer architecture, and even the structural reasons why human civilizations collapse.

28
00:01:50,720 --> 00:01:55,400
I mean, it sounds like a theory of everything, which, I have to admit, usually makes my skeptical alarm bells go off.

29
00:01:55,400 --> 00:01:57,720
Oh, for sure. It's a huge red flag normally.

30
00:01:57,820 --> 00:02:03,920
Right. Whenever someone claims they've solved quantum gravity, the human brain, and the fall of Rome in one single document,

31
00:02:04,200 --> 00:02:06,980
you have to wonder if they've just completely lost the plot.

32
00:02:07,120 --> 00:02:09,440
It sounds like crackpot territory at first glance.

33
00:02:09,760 --> 00:02:16,780
It really does. But the deeper I read into this, the more the underlying logic started to warp how I was looking at the room around me.

34
00:02:17,320 --> 00:02:19,520
So let's start at the absolute foundation.

35
00:02:20,080 --> 00:02:23,380
What is the core principle of this admissibility program?

36
00:02:23,380 --> 00:02:26,100
Like, what is the lens we are supposed to be looking through?

37
00:02:26,600 --> 00:02:30,620
So the central shift is moving away from a universe made of static objects.

38
00:02:30,760 --> 00:02:30,980
Okay.

39
00:02:31,660 --> 00:02:38,720
The traditional view, I mean, the one we navigate our daily lives with, is that reality is composed of nouns, things.

40
00:02:39,120 --> 00:02:41,680
You know, a chair, a planet, an electron.

41
00:02:41,920 --> 00:02:42,180
Right.

42
00:02:42,520 --> 00:02:47,720
But this program argues that reality is fundamentally made of what it calls admissible transformations.

43
00:02:48,000 --> 00:02:49,100
Admissible transformation.

44
00:02:49,100 --> 00:03:01,380
Yeah. The universe isn't a collection of objects sitting in empty space. It is a space of what is reachable or possible from any given point. It's a universe of processes and trajectories.

45
00:03:01,560 --> 00:03:07,060
I want to spend a lot of time on that because internalizing that idea completely rewrites how you look at the physical world.

46
00:03:07,060 --> 00:03:12,620
But before we get into the heavy physics and, like, the cosmology of it all, I want to talk about where this actually came from.

47
00:03:12,780 --> 00:03:14,080
The origin story is fascinating.

48
00:03:14,440 --> 00:03:23,720
Because you'd think an intellectual architecture that attempts to redefine gravity started with a bunch of theoretical physicists standing around a chalkboard, you know, doing algebraic geometry.

49
00:03:24,560 --> 00:03:25,480
But it did it.

50
00:03:25,480 --> 00:03:26,180
Far from it.

51
00:03:26,180 --> 00:03:33,880
The oldest, most foundational layer of this entire research program began in the physical, practical world.

52
00:03:34,400 --> 00:03:39,800
It was born out of construction, electrical work, plumbing, and property rehabilitation.

53
00:03:40,340 --> 00:03:42,280
I really struggled with this part at first.

54
00:03:42,580 --> 00:03:51,780
You're telling me a framework that attempts to explain the cosmological smoothing of the early universe was literally inspired by fixing leaky pipes.

55
00:03:51,780 --> 00:03:52,860
I know, it sounds absurd.

56
00:03:52,960 --> 00:03:56,340
I mean, physics is supposed to be about pristine universal laws.

57
00:03:56,780 --> 00:03:59,740
Plumbing is just patching things up in a basement.

58
00:03:59,740 --> 00:04:04,940
But that tension you're feeling is exactly the point the document makes about embodied knowledge.

59
00:04:05,080 --> 00:04:06,220
Okay, unpack that for me.

60
00:04:06,640 --> 00:04:10,500
Well, theoretical physics historically loves to start with a blank slate.

61
00:04:11,400 --> 00:04:18,180
They imagine, you know, a perfectly spherical cow in a frictionless vacuum, and then they write beautiful symmetrical equations about it.

62
00:04:18,200 --> 00:04:19,560
Right, the ideal scenario.

63
00:04:20,000 --> 00:04:20,540
Exactly.

64
00:04:20,540 --> 00:04:30,300
But when you are standing in a hundred-year-old house with a wrench, tasked with fixing a degraded plumbing system, you do not have a blank slate.

65
00:04:30,640 --> 00:04:31,780
No, definitely not.

66
00:04:32,000 --> 00:04:35,460
You can't just erase the house and build the mathematically ideal pipe system?

67
00:04:35,540 --> 00:04:40,520
Right, because if I knock out a load-bearing wall to put in the perfect pipe, the roof collapses.

68
00:04:41,020 --> 00:04:44,040
I'm constrained by the reality of the mess right in front of me.

69
00:04:44,340 --> 00:04:46,440
And that constraint is everything.

70
00:04:46,440 --> 00:04:51,100
You have to ask a fundamentally different question than the theoretical physicist asks.

71
00:04:51,520 --> 00:04:59,500
You look at the system and ask, what transformations are actually available from this specific state right now without causing a catastrophic failure?

72
00:04:59,620 --> 00:05:02,000
You are navigating an admissibility landscape.

73
00:05:02,000 --> 00:05:02,820
Precisely.

74
00:05:02,820 --> 00:05:09,640
And from that sustained, very messy engagement with physical repair, the author extracted four recurring intuitions.

75
00:05:10,100 --> 00:05:14,180
And these four principles formed the bedrock of the entire admissibility program.

76
00:05:14,180 --> 00:05:16,040
Let's break down those four intuitions.

77
00:05:16,140 --> 00:05:17,220
I have them here in my notes.

78
00:05:17,360 --> 00:05:18,960
First is process over object.

79
00:05:19,340 --> 00:05:21,340
Second, accessibility over location.

80
00:05:21,640 --> 00:05:21,840
Right.

81
00:05:22,020 --> 00:05:23,380
Third, repair over construction.

82
00:05:23,600 --> 00:05:25,680
And fourth, continuity over replacement.

83
00:05:25,680 --> 00:05:34,960
If you can just keep those four ideas in your head, the rest of the extremely dense math in this document becomes much, much easier to parse.

84
00:05:35,300 --> 00:05:38,400
Let me test an analogy on you to make sure I'm really getting this.

85
00:05:38,480 --> 00:05:39,880
Let's look at a game of chess.

86
00:05:40,020 --> 00:05:40,900
Okay, great example.

87
00:05:40,900 --> 00:05:47,160
If you look at reality the traditional way, you are looking at the wooden pieces on the board, the objects.

88
00:05:47,760 --> 00:05:49,040
You say, that is a knight.

89
00:05:49,300 --> 00:05:50,220
It is made of oak.

90
00:05:50,500 --> 00:05:51,880
It sits on square G1.

91
00:05:52,040 --> 00:05:52,580
The nouns.

92
00:05:52,740 --> 00:05:53,420
Right, the nouns.

93
00:05:53,420 --> 00:05:59,520
But if you look at reality through the lens of the admissibility program, you aren't looking at the wooden pieces at all, are you?

94
00:05:59,640 --> 00:06:01,300
No, you're looking at the negative space.

95
00:06:01,440 --> 00:06:01,820
Exactly.

96
00:06:02,160 --> 00:06:05,400
You are only looking at the invisible map of legal moves.

97
00:06:05,500 --> 00:06:06,940
You are looking at the reachability.

98
00:06:07,560 --> 00:06:10,720
The reality of the knight isn't the carved piece of wood.

99
00:06:11,260 --> 00:06:15,620
The reality of the knight is the L-shaped trajectory it is permitted to take.

100
00:06:15,760 --> 00:06:17,600
It's defined by what it can do next.

101
00:06:17,660 --> 00:06:17,860
Right.

102
00:06:18,260 --> 00:06:20,140
The game isn't the physical pieces.

103
00:06:20,140 --> 00:06:23,860
The game is the shifting web of admissible transformations.

104
00:06:24,360 --> 00:06:31,160
The physical piece is really just a convenient placeholder for the human eye to track a set of allowed behaviors.

105
00:06:31,520 --> 00:06:35,640
And for anyone listening right now, you can apply this to your own life.

106
00:06:35,760 --> 00:06:36,440
I was so...

107
00:06:36,440 --> 00:06:39,640
Think about how often we are sold the fantasy of the blank slate.

108
00:06:39,640 --> 00:06:47,740
We want to tear down our old habits or our old careers or our old societal structures and build a perfect new one from scratch.

109
00:06:47,980 --> 00:06:48,620
Oh, constantly.

110
00:06:48,880 --> 00:06:50,840
The whole, you know, new year, new me thing.

111
00:06:50,920 --> 00:06:51,320
Exactly.

112
00:06:51,800 --> 00:06:56,980
But the messy reality of living is that replacement is incredibly rare.

113
00:06:57,620 --> 00:07:04,000
We almost always have to focus on repair and maintenance to keep our lives functioning while we try to change them.

114
00:07:04,000 --> 00:07:08,120
That's a profound way to look at it, because we have this cultural obsession with the new.

115
00:07:08,680 --> 00:07:12,500
We view the need for repair as, like, a failure state.

116
00:07:12,600 --> 00:07:15,600
If something needs fixing, we think it's broken or obsolete.

117
00:07:16,120 --> 00:07:19,180
But in the admissibility program, needing repair is not a sign of failure.

118
00:07:19,740 --> 00:07:21,840
Repair is the fundamental category of existence.

119
00:07:22,280 --> 00:07:22,640
Wow.

120
00:07:22,980 --> 00:07:29,100
If something exists in this universe, it requires continuous repair against entropy just to maintain its shape.

121
00:07:29,100 --> 00:07:38,800
So if we take this plumber's intuition, that verbs matter more than nouns, and that the map of legal moves is more real than the wooden chess pieces,

122
00:07:39,440 --> 00:07:43,000
how does that actually translate into a scientific philosophy?

123
00:07:43,560 --> 00:07:47,000
It translates into what the document calls the reachability ontology.

124
00:07:47,380 --> 00:07:49,640
This is the philosophical engine of the whole project.

125
00:07:49,740 --> 00:07:49,900
Okay.

126
00:07:50,020 --> 00:07:51,260
The reachability ontology.

127
00:07:51,440 --> 00:07:51,620
Yeah.

128
00:07:51,800 --> 00:07:55,700
It formally claims that reality is not composed of static entities.

129
00:07:55,700 --> 00:08:00,460
It's composed entirely of reachable trajectories and accessible futures.

130
00:08:00,760 --> 00:08:04,820
See, I need to push back here, because my brain is actively resisting this.

131
00:08:04,860 --> 00:08:07,260
Like, I'm looking at the coffee mug on the desk in front of me.

132
00:08:07,380 --> 00:08:07,600
Okay.

133
00:08:07,800 --> 00:08:08,780
It's solid ceramic.

134
00:08:08,980 --> 00:08:09,720
It has mass.

135
00:08:09,920 --> 00:08:10,740
It reflects light.

136
00:08:10,840 --> 00:08:12,300
It is very clearly a thing.

137
00:08:12,500 --> 00:08:13,300
I can pick it up.

138
00:08:13,540 --> 00:08:15,240
Are you telling me this mug is a verb?

139
00:08:15,420 --> 00:08:18,160
The text has a very specific term for what you're holding.

140
00:08:18,400 --> 00:08:20,540
It calls your mug a frozen process.

141
00:08:20,780 --> 00:08:21,720
A frozen process?

142
00:08:22,020 --> 00:08:22,180
Yeah.

143
00:08:22,760 --> 00:08:24,460
Think about the time frame of your perception.

144
00:08:24,460 --> 00:08:30,180
To a fruit fly, a human being standing still might look like a mountain, you know, a permanent

145
00:08:30,180 --> 00:08:31,100
geographic feature.

146
00:08:31,180 --> 00:08:32,360
Because their lives are so short.

147
00:08:32,680 --> 00:08:32,960
Right.

